# Burn

Burn is a **next-generation deep learning framework** built entirely in Rust. It's designed from the ground up to be flexible, efficient, and portable — capable of training and deploying neural networks across GPUs, CPUs, browsers, and even embedded devices without changing your model code.

Unlike frameworks that bolt on Rust bindings to C++ libraries, Burn is native Rust all the way down, giving you compile-time safety, fearless concurrency, and zero-cost abstractions for deep learning.

## Why Burn?

| | Burn | PyTorch | TensorFlow |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Language** | Native Rust | Python/C++ | Python/C++ |
| **Type Safety** | Compile-time tensor checks | Runtime errors | Runtime errors |
| **Memory** | No GC, ownership model | GC + manual management | GC |
| **Browser Deploy** | WASM + WebGPU native | Not supported | TF.js (separate API) |
| **Embedded/no_std** | Supported | Not supported | TF Lite (separate API) |
| **Backend Switching** | One line change | Rewrite needed | Rewrite needed |
| **JIT Compilation** | Built-in kernel fusion | TorchScript/Dynamo | XLA |

## Key Features

- **Multi-Backend Architecture** — Write your model once, run it on any hardware. Switch backends with a single type parameter.
- **JIT Kernel Fusion** — Burn's CubeCL compiler automatically fuses tensor operations into optimized GPU kernels at runtime
- **Auto-Tuning** — Runtime search finds the fastest execution strategy for your specific hardware
- **Dynamic Graphs** — Full dynamic computation graphs with static-graph-level performance
- **Built-in Training Dashboard** — Terminal UI (via Ratatui) with real-time metrics, loss curves, and graceful interruption
- **ONNX Import** — Convert PyTorch/TensorFlow ONNX models to native Rust code
- **Quantization** — Post-training quantization in 8-bit, 4-bit, and 2-bit formats
- **SafeTensors & PyTorch Weights** — Load pretrained model weights directly

## Supported Backends

| Backend | Hardware | Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| **CUDA** | NVIDIA GPUs | Maximum training/inference performance |
| **ROCm** | AMD GPUs | AMD GPU acceleration |
| **Metal** | Apple Silicon | Mac/iOS native GPU |
| **Vulkan** | Cross-platform GPU | Portable GPU compute |
| **WebGPU (WGPU)** | Browser GPU | In-browser GPU inference |
| **LibTorch** | CPU/GPU | PyTorch interop |
| **NdArray** | CPU | Lightweight CPU inference |
| **Flex** | CPU | no_std / embedded |
| **Candle** | CPU/GPU | Lightweight alternative |

### Backend Decorators

Burn's backends are composable — wrap any backend with decorators to add capabilities:

- **Autodiff** — Adds automatic differentiation for training (type-safe: `.backward()` only exists on autodiff backends)
- **Fusion** — Automatically fuses operations into optimized kernels
- **Router** — Run some operations on GPU, others on CPU in the same model
- **Remote** — Distribute computation across machines over the network

## Installation

```bash
cargo add burn --features wgpu   # GPU via WebGPU/Vulkan
# or
cargo add burn --features cuda    # NVIDIA CUDA
# or
cargo add burn --features ndarray # CPU only
```

## Quick Start — Define a Model

Burn models are regular Rust structs that derive `Module`:

```rust
use burn::prelude::*;
use burn::nn::{Linear, LinearConfig, Relu};

#[derive(Module, Debug)]
pub struct MnistModel<B: Backend> {
    linear1: Linear<B>,
    linear2: Linear<B>,
    activation: Relu,
}

impl<B: Backend> MnistModel<B> {
    pub fn new(device: &B::Device) -> Self {
        let linear1 = LinearConfig::new(784, 256).init(device);
        let linear2 = LinearConfig::new(256, 10).init(device);
        Self {
            linear1,
            linear2,
            activation: Relu::new(),
        }
    }

    pub fn forward(&self, input: Tensor<B, 2>) -> Tensor<B, 2> {
        let x = self.linear1.forward(input);
        let x = self.activation.forward(x);
        self.linear2.forward(x)
    }
}
```

The `B: Backend` generic means this exact model runs on CUDA, WebGPU, CPU, or in the browser — no code changes.

## Training

Burn provides a complete training infrastructure with a `Learner` that handles the training loop, metrics, checkpointing, and a live terminal dashboard:

```rust
use burn::train::{LearnerBuilder, ClassificationOutput};
use burn::train::metric::{AccuracyMetric, LossMetric};
use burn::optim::AdamConfig;
use burn::lr_scheduler::linear::LinearLrSchedulerConfig;

let learner = LearnerBuilder::new("/tmp/burn-mnist")
    .metric_train_numeric(AccuracyMetric::new())
    .metric_valid_numeric(AccuracyMetric::new())
    .metric_train_numeric(LossMetric::new())
    .metric_valid_numeric(LossMetric::new())
    .num_epochs(10)
    .build(
        model,
        AdamConfig::new().init(),
        LinearLrSchedulerConfig::new(1e-3, 1e-5, 10).init(),
    );

let trained_model = learner.fit(train_loader, valid_loader);
```

## Inference — Switch Backends in One Line

```rust
// Training on CUDA with autodiff
type TrainBackend = burn::backend::Autodiff<burn::backend::Cuda>;
let model = MnistModel::<TrainBackend>::new(&device);

// Inference on CPU — same model, just change the type
type InferBackend = burn::backend::NdArray;
let model = model.into::<InferBackend>();

// Or in the browser via WebGPU
type WebBackend = burn::backend::Wgpu;
let model = model.into::<WebBackend>();
```

## ONNX Model Import

Convert any ONNX model (exported from PyTorch, TensorFlow, etc.) into native Burn code:

```rust
// In build.rs
use burn_import::onnx::ModelGen;

fn main() {
    ModelGen::new()
        .input("src/model/my_model.onnx")
        .out_dir("model/")
        .run_from_script();
}

// In your code — it's now a native Burn model
mod model {
    include!(concat!(env!("OUT_DIR"), "/model/my_model.rs"));
}

let model = model::Model::<Backend>::default();
let output = model.forward(input_tensor);
```

## Loading PyTorch Weights

Reuse pretrained PyTorch or SafeTensors weights in your Burn models:

```rust
use burn::record::{FullPrecisionSettings, NamedMpkFileRecorder};

// Load from SafeTensors format
let record = NamedMpkFileRecorder::<FullPrecisionSettings>::default()
    .load("pretrained_weights".into(), &device)?;

let model = MnistModel::<Backend>::new(&device).load_record(record);
```

## Tensor Operations

Burn's tensor API is expressive and type-safe:

```rust
use burn::prelude::*;

// Create tensors
let x = Tensor::<Backend, 2>::random([3, 4], Distribution::Default, &device);
let y = Tensor::<Backend, 2>::ones([4, 5], &device);

// Operations
let z = x.matmul(y);               // Matrix multiply
let z = z.relu();                   // Activation
let z = z.softmax(1);               // Softmax along dim 1
let z = z.reshape([3, 1, 5]);       // Reshape
let z = z.slice([0..2, 0..1, 0..3]); // Slicing

// Autodiff (only available on Autodiff<B> backends)
let grads = z.sum().backward();
let x_grad = x.grad(&grads);       // Gradient w.r.t. x
```

## Built-in Layers

Burn ships with all the standard neural network building blocks:

| Category | Layers |
|---|---|
| **Linear** | Linear, Bilinear |
| **Conv** | Conv1d, Conv2d, Conv3d, ConvTranspose1d/2d/3d |
| **Normalization** | BatchNorm, LayerNorm, GroupNorm, RmsNorm, InstanceNorm |
| **Pooling** | MaxPool1d/2d, AvgPool1d/2d, AdaptiveAvgPool1d/2d |
| **Recurrent** | LSTM, GRU, BiLSTM, BiGRU |
| **Attention** | MultiHeadAttention |
| **Embedding** | Embedding |
| **Activation** | Relu, Gelu, Sigmoid, Tanh, LeakyRelu, Mish, and more |
| **Regularization** | Dropout |
| **Loss** | CrossEntropy, MSE, Huber, Binary Cross Entropy |

## Deploy to the Browser

Burn compiles to WebAssembly with GPU acceleration via WebGPU:

```rust
// Same model code, compiled to WASM
// Use the Wgpu backend for GPU inference in-browser
// or Flex/NdArray for CPU-only WASM

#[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]
type Backend = burn::backend::Wgpu;

#[cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))]
type Backend = burn::backend::Cuda;
```

Burn includes working examples of MNIST digit recognition and image classification running entirely in the browser.

## Deploy to Embedded (no_std)

With the `Flex` backend, Burn runs on bare-metal embedded systems — no heap allocator required for inference.

