# Rig

Rig is an open-source Rust library for building portable, modular, and lightweight **fullstack AI agents**. It provides high-level abstractions for LLM completion and embedding workflows while giving you full control over the underlying behavior — with minimal boilerplate.

Used in production by organizations like **St. Jude Children's Research Hospital** (genomics visualization), **Nethermind**, **Neon**, and **Dria** (decentralized AI network).

## Why Rig?

| | Rig (Rust) | Python Frameworks |
|---|---|---|
| **Performance** | Native speed, zero-cost abstractions | Interpreter overhead |
| **Safety** | Compile-time type checking catches LLM output mismatches | Runtime errors in production |
| **Memory** | No GC, minimal footprint for agent swarms | High memory per process |
| **Portability** | Compiles to WASM for browser-side agents | Server-only |
| **Concurrency** | Tokio async runtime, fearless concurrency | GIL limitations |

## Key Features

- **20+ Model Providers** — OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Cohere, Ollama, DeepSeek, Groq, Mistral, Perplexity, xAI, Azure, HuggingFace, OpenRouter, Together, Voyage AI, and more under a unified interface
- **10+ Vector Store Integrations** — MongoDB, LanceDB, Neo4j, Qdrant, SQLite, SurrealDB, Milvus, ScyllaDB, AWS S3Vectors, HelixDB
- **Typed Tool Use** — Define tools with automatic JSON schema generation via `#[tool_macro]`
- **RAG Pipelines** — First-class retrieval-augmented generation with embeddings and vector search
- **Agentic Workflows** — Multi-turn streaming, tool calling, and multi-step reasoning
- **Structured Extraction** — Pull typed data from unstructured text
- **Audio & Image Generation** — Transcription, audio generation, and image generation models
- **OpenTelemetry** — Built-in tracing with GenAI Semantic Convention and Langfuse compatibility
- **Full WASM Support** — Run agents in the browser

## Installation

```bash
cargo add rig-core
```

For vector store integrations, add companion crates:

```bash
cargo add rig-lancedb   # LanceDB
cargo add rig-mongodb   # MongoDB
cargo add rig-qdrant    # Qdrant
cargo add rig-neo4j     # Neo4j
cargo add rig-sqlite    # SQLite
```

## Quick Start — Your First Agent

Create a simple conversational agent in under 10 lines:

```rust
use rig::providers::openai;
use rig::completion::Prompt;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), anyhow::Error> {
    let client = openai::Client::from_env();

    let agent = client
        .agent("gpt-4o")
        .preamble("You are a helpful Rust programming assistant.")
        .build();

    let response = agent.prompt("Explain ownership in Rust").await?;
    println!("{response}");

    Ok(())
}
```

## Tool Use

Define typed tools that your agent can call. Rig automatically generates the JSON schema and handles serialization:

```rust
use rig::tool::Tool;
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};

#[derive(Deserialize)]
pub struct SearchArgs {
    pub query: String,
    pub max_results: Option<usize>,
}

#[derive(Serialize)]
pub struct SearchResult {
    pub title: String,
    pub url: String,
    pub snippet: String,
}

#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
#[error("Search error")]
pub struct SearchError;

pub struct WebSearch;

impl Tool for WebSearch {
    const NAME: &'static str = "web_search";
    type Args = SearchArgs;
    type Output = Vec<SearchResult>;
    type Error = SearchError;

    async fn definition(&self, _prompt: String) -> rig::tool::ToolDefinition {
        rig::tool::ToolDefinition {
            name: "web_search".to_string(),
            description: "Search the web for information".to_string(),
            parameters: serde_json::json!({
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                    "query": { "type": "string", "description": "Search query" },
                    "max_results": { "type": "integer", "description": "Max results" }
                },
                "required": ["query"]
            }),
        }
    }

    async fn call(&self, args: Self::Args) -> Result<Self::Output, Self::Error> {
        // Your search implementation here
        Ok(vec![SearchResult {
            title: format!("Result for: {}", args.query),
            url: "https://example.com".into(),
            snippet: "Relevant information...".into(),
        }])
    }
}
```

Then attach it to your agent:

```rust
let agent = client
    .agent("gpt-4o")
    .preamble("You are a research assistant with web access.")
    .tool(WebSearch)
    .build();

let response = agent.prompt("Find the latest Rust release notes").await?;
```

## RAG — Retrieval-Augmented Generation

Rig makes RAG a first-class pattern. Embed documents, store them in a vector database, and query with semantic search:

```rust
use rig::providers::openai;
use rig::vector_store::VectorStoreIndex;
use rig::embeddings::EmbeddingsBuilder;

let client = openai::Client::from_env();
let embedding_model = client.embedding_model("text-embedding-3-small");

// Create embeddings from your documents
let embeddings = EmbeddingsBuilder::new(embedding_model.clone())
    .document("Rust was first released in 2015.")?
    .document("Rust uses a borrow checker for memory safety.")?
    .document("Cargo is Rust's package manager.")?
    .build()
    .await?;

// Store in your vector database of choice
let index = store.add_documents(embeddings).await?;

// Build a RAG agent that searches your knowledge base
let rag_agent = client
    .agent("gpt-4o")
    .preamble("Answer questions using the provided context.")
    .dynamic_context(2, index)  // retrieve top 2 relevant docs
    .build();

let answer = rag_agent
    .prompt("How does Rust handle memory safety?")
    .await?;
```

## Streaming

Stream responses token-by-token for real-time UIs:

```rust
use rig::completion::Chat;
use futures::StreamExt;

let mut stream = agent.stream_prompt("Write a haiku about Rust").await?;

while let Some(chunk) = stream.next().await {
    match chunk {
        Ok(text) => print!("{text}"),
        Err(e) => eprintln!("Error: {e}"),
    }
}
```

## Structured Data Extraction

Extract typed Rust structs from unstructured text:

```rust
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use schemars::JsonSchema;

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize, Serialize, JsonSchema)]
struct ContactInfo {
    name: String,
    email: Option<String>,
    phone: Option<String>,
    company: Option<String>,
}

let extractor = client
    .extractor::<ContactInfo>("gpt-4o")
    .preamble("Extract contact information from the text.")
    .build();

let contact: ContactInfo = extractor
    .extract("Hi, I'm Jane Smith from Acme Corp. Reach me at jane@acme.com")
    .await?;

println!("Name: {}", contact.name);       // Jane Smith
println!("Email: {:?}", contact.email);    // Some("jane@acme.com")
println!("Company: {:?}", contact.company); // Some("Acme Corp")
```

## Multi-Provider Support

Switch between providers without changing your application logic:

```rust
use rig::providers::{openai, anthropic};

// OpenAI
let openai_client = openai::Client::from_env();
let gpt = openai_client.agent("gpt-4o").preamble("You help with code.").build();

// Anthropic
let anthropic_client = anthropic::Client::from_env();
let claude = anthropic_client.agent("claude-sonnet-4-20250514").preamble("You help with code.").build();

// Ollama (local models)
use rig::providers::ollama;
let ollama_client = ollama::Client::new();
let local = ollama_client.agent("llama3").preamble("You help with code.").build();

// Same interface for all three
let response = gpt.prompt("Fix this bug").await?;
let response = claude.prompt("Fix this bug").await?;
let response = local.prompt("Fix this bug").await?;
```

## Vector Store Integrations

Rig supports 10+ vector stores through companion crates:

| Vector Store | Crate | Best For |
|---|---|---|
| **MongoDB** | `rig-mongodb` | Existing MongoDB deployments |
| **LanceDB** | `rig-lancedb` | Embedded, serverless vector search |
| **Qdrant** | `rig-qdrant` | Dedicated vector database at scale |
| **Neo4j** | `rig-neo4j` | Graph + vector hybrid queries |
| **SQLite** | `rig-sqlite` | Lightweight, embedded applications |
| **SurrealDB** | `rig-surrealdb` | Multi-model database |
| **Milvus** | `rig-milvus` | High-performance similarity search |
| **ScyllaDB** | `rig-scylladb` | Low-latency at massive scale |
| **AWS S3Vectors** | `rig-s3vectors` | AWS-native vector storage |
| **HelixDB** | `rig-helixdb` | Graph-native vector database |

## Architecture

Rig is built around a few core traits that you can implement for custom integrations:

- **`CompletionModel`** — Implement for any LLM provider
- **`EmbeddingModel`** — Implement for any embedding service
- **`VectorStoreIndex`** — Implement for any vector database
- **`Tool`** — Define callable tools for agents

This trait-based design means Rig is infinitely extensible. If your provider or database isn't supported yet, you can add it yourself.

